<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bahram V</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 438</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Bahram Gur</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bahram V was a Sasanian king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> who reigned from 420 to 438. He fought a short war with the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> in 421 and 422 and defended the north-eastern frontier against invading nomads. Famous as a hunter, he became a hero of Persian literature as Bahram Gur, celebrated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdowsi">Ferdowsi</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami</a>'s Seven Beauties. His coins were widely imitated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bahram-v.jpg" alt="Bahram V" width="320" height="325" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plate_with_a_hunting_scene_from_the_tale_of_Bahram_Gur_and_Azadeh_MET_DT1634.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bahram was born around 400, the son of the Sasanian shah Yazdegerd I. As a boy he was sent to the Lakhmid court at al-Hira, where the king al-Nu'man I provided teachers from the Sasanian court to instruct him in law, archery and horsemanship. When Yazdegerd I was murdered in 420, the nobles and priests elected Khosrow, a son of Bahram IV, as shah.</p><p>Bahram refused this and asked for help from the Lakhmid king al-Mundhir I, who gave him an army. According to a long-standing legend in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a>, he proposed that the royal crown and robes be placed between two lions and that whoever retrieved them should be shah. Khosrow withdrew and Bahram won the throne. Distrusting the nobles, he turned to the Zoroastrian priesthood and was the first Sasanian shah crowned by a chief priest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He began his reign with a persecution of the Christians. Refugees fled to Roman territory, and when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> asked for their return <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a> declared war instead. In 421 the Roman general Ardaburius entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and defeated the Iranian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a>, then besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nisibis">Nisibis</a> but withdrew when Bahram advanced. The peace of 422 restored the earlier position, with both sides agreeing to guarantee freedom of religion in their territories.</p><p>While Bahram was occupied in the west, the Kidarites crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a>, took the rich city of Marw and reached as far as Ray. After the peace with Rome Bahram marched to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a>, where his forces routed the Kidarites and killed their king. The war ended in 427. He set up a pillar at the Oxus to mark it as his eastern frontier, and the Sogdian city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> later struck coins with his image. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> became a frontier province governed by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marzban">marzban</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bahram died in 438 and his manner of death is unclear. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdowsi">Ferdowsi</a> says he died in his sleep, while Nizami and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-khusrau">Amir Khusrau</a> say he vanished in a cave while chasing an onager. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazdegerd-ii">Yazdegerd II</a> succeeded him. He is remembered for cancelling taxes and debts at celebrations, for encouraging musicians, and for his love of hunting, which gave him the epithet Gur, 'onager'. Nizami's romance Haft Peykar, of 1197, tells of the seven princesses who became his wives.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bahram V” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their silver coins, imitating the Sasanian king <b>Bahram V</b>, circulated widely as 'Bukhar Khudah drachms' for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-bukhara">Principality of Bukhara</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/media-region">Media</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37,47&amp;year=428" aria-label="Media on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Bahram V"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4120 4353h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4120 4353h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=37,47&amp;year=428">What was here in 428 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
